  "query_preview": "..\nSaturday morning and afternoon: Conditions will remain dry\nthrough much of the day Saturday with afternoon high\ntemperatures in the 70s for most locations. Mid and high level\nclouds will increase ahead of an incoming low pressure system, \nbringing overcast skies at times. As the low approaches, a\ntightening east to northeast pressure gradient will result in\nbreezy northeast winds with sustained speeds of 10-15 mph and\ngusts around 25 mph across the Idaho Panhandle, West Plains,\nand Columbia Basin.\n\nSaturday night through Sunday night: The aforementioned upper-\nlevel low pressure system will track inland, ushering in a\nstream of moisture-rich air. Strong synoptic forcing combined\nwith the saturated airmass will bring much-needed widespread \nrainfall across the Inland Northwest. Latest guidance has\nshifted the heaviest precipitation amounts west and north with\nthe focus now over Central WA. It's worth noting that models \nhave been inconsistent in their predictions of the track of low\nand the location of the heaviest precip amounts over the past\nseveral days, so it's very possible that the predictions will\nchange again.\n\nThere is high confidence that most locations will see \nmeasurable precip, but significant uncertainty remains regarding\ntotal precip amounts. Overall, rainfall amounts have continued\nto trend higher, but there are still substantial differences \nbetween the high- end and low- end amounts predicted by the \nmodels. For example, model runs from this time yesterday showed \nthe 10th percentile (low- end) rain amount forecast for Spokane \nwas 0.1 inches, while the 90th percentile (high- end) amount \nwas 1.4 inches. Today's model runs have increased the low-end\namount to 0.4 inches and the high-end amount to 1.5 inches. \nStill a big range!\n\nThe NBM's chances for 1 inch or more of rain from Saturday \nevening through Monday morning are as follows:\n\n- 50-80% for Central WA\n- 40-60% for Eastern WA\n- 40-70% for ID Panhandle\n\nTemperatures Sunday will be well below normal in the 50s and \n60s. \n\nMonday through Thursday: High pressure builds into the region as\nthe weekend system exits to the east. Temperatures will \ngradually return to near seasonal normals in the 70s to low 80s \nby late next week with dry conditions expected. Fog potential \nwill be something to monitor Monday morning with a moist \nboundary layer following all of the weekend rain. /Fewkes",
